{"input_sha256":"28ebb1a5204adb29ab1a3313e44f51467580565a954be559c0b302b0967b23c2","problem_sha256":"eef5a1f83bb66d5156d89eceaee6f6dbc225d8de799acbd8424421bffb19003b","read_receipts":[{"object_id":"obj_41923f60389eb9cfe812ae7cf74bc64d6a09027a5ec2002f752a4bfa86b169ed","sha256":"117ef251ee43b41f7dcb29139f11d552d80895fce9529af10552bcf64ce6d225","media_type":"application/json","size_bytes":4177}],"usage":{"provider_requests":null,"total_tokens":null},"output":{"lemma_proposals":[{"statement":"Let m,n be coprime integers with n>0, mn≠0, and D=m²−n²≠0. Put H=m²+n² and define a=−9H/(8D), b=(m²−7n²)H(7m²−n²)/(8D³), and c=8D³/(9H³). Then a, b, and c are all nonintegers.","assumptions":["m,n are coprime integers, n>0, mn≠0, and m²≠n²; equivalently t=m/n is rational and t∉{0,1,−1}.","The three coordinates are exactly a=−9H/(8D), b=(m²−7n²)H(7m²−n²)/(8D³), c=8D³/(9H³), where H=m²+n² and D=m²−n²."],"quantifiers":["For every pair of integers m,n satisfying the assumptions, with no height bound."],"proof":"Because gcd(m,n)=1, either m,n have opposite parity or both are odd. The factors m²−7n² and 7m²−n² are nonzero: either vanishing would imply that a rational square is 7 or 1/7, both impossible (for example, comparing the exponent of 7 in a reduced square equation gives an odd exponent on one side). If m,n have opposite parity, H,D and both factors are odd, so v₂(a)=v₂(b)=−3. If both are odd, their squares are 1 modulo 8, so v₂(H)=1, v₂(D)≥3, and m²−7n²≡2 and 7m²−n²≡6 modulo 8. Thus v₂(a)=1−3−v₂(D)<0 and v₂(b)=1+1+1−3−3v₂(D)=−3v₂(D)<0. In either case a and b are nonintegers, since an integer has nonnegative 2-adic valuation. Finally, H+D=2m²>0 and H−D=2n²>0, so 0<|D|<H. Hence 0<|c|=(8/9)(|D|/H)³<8/9<1, and c is not an integer.","dependency_ids":[],"source_candidate_id":"cand_809b6896a53349afa46e58ba9a69c455","external_results":[]},{"statement":"For coprime integers m,n with n>0, mn≠0, and m²≠n², put H=m²+n², D=m²−n², and Q=m⁴+34m²n²+n⁴. Define a=−9H/(8D), b=(m²−7n²)H(7m²−n²)/(8D³), c=8D³/(9H³), e=−2(m²+5n²)(5m²+n²)/(9DH), f=−2mn(m²−4mn−3n²)(3m²−4mn−n²)(m³+8m²n+5mn²+4n³)(4m³−5m²n+8mn²−n³)/(DHQ²), and g=2mn(m²+4mn−3n²)(3m²+4mn−n²)(m³−8m²n+5mn²−4n³)(4m³+5m²n+8mn²+n³)/(DHQ²). Then none of a,b,c,e,f,g is an integer. Moreover, Q has a prime divisor p>3; every prime p>3 dividing Q satisfies p≡1 (mod 8) and vₚ(f)=vₚ(g)=−2vₚ(Q).","assumptions":["m,n are coprime integers with n>0, mn≠0, and m²≠n²; these are the reduced-parameter conditions for rational t outside {0,1,−1}.","H=m²+n², D=m²−n², Q=m⁴+34m²n²+n⁴, and the six rational coordinates are exactly the formulas stated in the lemma statement."],"quantifiers":["For every pair of integers m,n satisfying the assumptions, with no height bound.","For every prime p>3 dividing the corresponding positive integer Q."],"proof":"This is a newly reconstructed proof of the supplied host-authored candidate argument; it is not an assertion that the earlier all-six candidate was proved or accepted. First, for a and b use the parity argument in the preceding proposal: opposite parity gives v₂(a)=v₂(b)=−3; when both m,n are odd, v₂(H)=1, v₂(D)≥3, and the two factors m²−7n² and 7m²−n² have valuations 1, giving v₂(a)=1−3−v₂(D)<0 and v₂(b)=−3v₂(D)<0. All factors are nonzero, as shown there. Since 0<|D|<H, the formula for c gives 0<|c|<8/9, so c is not an integer.

If e were an integer, its displayed numerator N=−2(m²+5n²)(5m²+n²) would be divisible by 9DH, hence H would divide N. Modulo H, m²≡−n², so N≡32n⁴ (mod H). Also gcd(H,n)=1, so H|32. But H is positive, and its 2-adic valuation is 0 if m,n have opposite parity and 1 if both are odd. A positive divisor of 32 with either valuation is 1 or 2, whereas H=m²+n²≥5 because m,n are nonzero and |m|≠n. Contradiction. Thus e is not an integer.

We establish a prime divisor of Q beyond 2 and 3. No prime dividing Q divides m or n, since reducing Q modulo such a prime would give the nonzero fourth power of the other, using gcd(m,n)=1. If m,n have opposite parity then Q is odd. If both are odd, their fourth powers are 1 modulo 16 and m²n² is 1 or 9 modulo 16; since 34≡2 modulo 16, Q≡4 modulo 16. Thus v₂(Q) is respectively 0 or 2. If 3|mn, coprimality gives Q≡1 modulo 3. Otherwise m²≡n²≡1 modulo 3 and D=3k for an integer k. The identity Q=D²+36m²n² gives Q=9(k²+4m²n²), where the parenthesis is nonzero modulo 3, so v₃(Q)=2. If Q had no prime divisor beyond 2 and 3, these bounds would imply Q|36. But at least one of |m|,n is at least 2 and the other at least 1, whence Q≥16+34·4+1=153. Therefore some prime p>3 divides Q.

To prove the necessary numerator factors do not cancel at p, set q(T)=T⁴+34T²+1 and define P₁=T²−4T−3, P₂=3T²−4T−1, P₃=T³+8T²+5T+4, P₄=4T³−5T²+8T−1. The following polynomial identities hold in Z[T]:
q+(-T³−34T)T=1;
(33−7T)q+(7T³−5T²+239T−181)P₁=576;
(197−123T)q+(41T³−11T²+1393T−379)P₂=576;
(17T²+123T−20)q+(-17T³+13T²−577T+437)P₃=1728;
(628T²−717T+1151)q+(-157T³−17T²−5333T−577)P₄=1728.
For exact coefficient checks, the pairs of coefficient vectors of the two summands in the last four identities, in ascending-degree order, are respectively [33,−7,1122,−238,33,−7]+[543,7,−1122,238,−33,7], [197,−123,6698,−4182,197,−123]+[379,123,−6698,4182,−197,123], [−20,123,−663,4182,558,123,17]+[1748,−123,663,−4182,−558,−123,−17], and [1151,−717,39762,−24378,22503,−717,628]+[577,717,−39762,24378,−22503,717,−628]. Each pair sums to the coefficient vector of the indicated constant. These checks follow by multiplying the displayed polynomials and collecting coefficients.

Fix p>3 dividing Q. Since p divides neither m nor n, T=m/n is defined in Fₚ and q(T)=Q/n⁴=0. The four identities, whose constants 576 and 1728 are nonzero modulo p, imply Pᵢ(T)≠0 for each i. Also T≠0. Homogenization multiplies the respective Pᵢ evaluations by n² or n³, units modulo p. Thus every factor in the numerator of f is nonzero modulo p. From Q=D²+36m²n² and Q=H²+32m²n², and p∤mn with p>3, it follows that p divides neither D nor H. The denominator DHQ² therefore has p-valuation 2vₚ(Q), while the numerator has valuation zero. Consequently vₚ(f)=−2vₚ(Q)<0 and f is nonzero and nonintegral.

Direct substitution in the displayed formulas gives g(m,n)=f(−m,n): the prefactor changes sign, each quadratic becomes its corresponding plus factor, and each cubic changes to the negative of its corresponding cubic, so the two cubic signs cancel. The hypotheses remain valid for (−m,n), and H,D,Q are unchanged. The same prime p argument applied to (−m,n) proves vₚ(g)=−2vₚ(Q)<0, so g is also nonintegral.

Finally, in Fₚ define r=D/(6mn) and s=H/(4mn); their denominators are units. The two identities for Q above yield r²=−1 and s²=−2. Thus u=s/r satisfies u²=2. Put z=u(1+r)/2. Then z²=2(1+2r+r²)/4=r, so z⁴=−1 and z⁸=1. As p is odd, −1≠1, and z has order exactly 8. The order of an element of Fₚ* divides p−1, hence p≡1 modulo 8; in particular p≥17. This theorem concerns only the displayed rational family and does not settle either the unrestricted-sign or all-positive sextuple existence question.","dependency_ids":[],"source_candidate_id":"cand_74c2d71b8b874b79acf7dc0a6ea6bc91","external_results":[]}]}}
